Lion's Mane (Hericium erinaceus) is a white mushroom covered in cascading “spines” that really does resemble a lion's mane. In East Asia it has been eaten for centuries, both as food and in traditional medicine. In recent decades it has become a focus of modern science.

## What compounds does it contain?

Lion's Mane contains two unique groups of compounds: hericenones (in the fruiting body) and erinacines (in the mycelium). In laboratory studies these compounds are investigated in connection with the synthesis of nerve growth factor (NGF) — which is why the mushroom is linked to cognitive function.

- Beta-glucans — to support the immune system
- Hericenones — the main subject of nervous-system research
- Protein, B vitamins and minerals

## Who is it for?

Lion's Mane is often chosen by students during exam season, by people doing intensive mental work, and by older adults looking for natural ways to support their memory.

> Functional mushrooms are not a miracle — they are a daily ritual whose effects you notice over weeks.

## How to take it properly

- Start with 1 g a day and gradually increase to 2 g
- Stir it into your morning coffee or tea — you'll barely taste it
- Take it regularly for at least 4–6 weeks
- Take a 1–2 week break after each course

Remember: Lion's Mane is a dietary supplement, not a medicine. If you take any medication or have a chronic condition, be sure to consult your doctor first.
